 Q3.3  INDEX VIEW & TABLE LOOP   [15 marks]   Pointer 5: Razor Views (loops, displaying records)
=====================================================================================

 LESSON
 ------
 The Index action did  return View(patients);  That list arrives in the view as the variable Model.
 To make Model know its type, declare it on line 1 with @model.
 A list of things is  IEnumerable<T>  (that's what the paper uses), so:
      @model IEnumerable<LibraryApp.Models.Book>

 Then you loop with @foreach and print each value with @item.Something. One <tr> per record.

 WORKED EXAMPLE (a table of Books)
 ---------------------------------
   @model IEnumerable<LibraryApp.Models.Book>

   <h2>Book List</h2>

   <table class="table">
       <thead>
           <tr>
               <th>Title</th>
               <th>Pages</th>
           </tr>
       </thead>
       <tbody>
           @foreach (var item in Model)
           {
               <tr>
                   <td>@item.Title</td>
                   <td>@item.Pages</td>
               </tr>
           }
       </tbody>
   </table>

 The @ before foreach flips you from HTML into C#. Inside the braces, HTML starts again the moment
 you write a tag like <tr>, then @item.Title flips back for one value.

 COMMON TRAPS
 ------------
 * <tr> must sit INSIDE the foreach braces, or you only get one row, or none.
 * @model (lowercase m, the type declaration) is not the same as Model (the data). Capital M for data.
 * The headings (<th>) go in <thead>, outside the loop. If they're inside you repeat them every row.
 * @item.DoctorId prints the number. To print the doctor's NAME you would need Include (bonus B3).
 * A foreach over a null list crashes. The controller gives you a list, so empty list = zero rows,
   no crash.

 THE TASK
 --------
 Write the Razor view for the Patient Index page. The view receives a list of patients:
   (a) set the model to IEnumerable of Patient
   (b) a heading "Patient List" and an HTML table with a header row:  Full Name | Age | Doctor ID
   (c) a @foreach loop over Model, one table row per patient
   (d) show FullName, Age and DoctorId in table cells
   (e) close the table
   Optional nicety (not marked): an "Add Patient" link to the Create action. Try
   <a asp-action="Create">. If you do add one, remember asp-action is a tag helper too.
   Also show the TempData["SuccessMessage"] on this page, because after a successful Create
   the app REDIRECTS here, so this is where the user actually sees the message.

